00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.156
The other the fourth strategy is to optimize your prompts.

00:10:03.156 --> 00:10:09.516
And the main idea again here is that if you
have shorter prompts, this means fewer input tokens.

00:10:09.516 --> 00:10:13.036
And this results into lower cost and faster execution.

00:10:13.116 --> 00:10:15.836
So how can we optimize our prompts?

00:10:16.076 --> 00:10:20.876
Here are a few ways. The first one is that not to be too prescriptive.

00:10:21.316 --> 00:10:24.036
In the older days, when the models were not so smart,

00:10:24.036 --> 00:10:29.556
you had to be very explicitly prescriptive when it comes
to specifically to negative steering.

00:10:29.596 --> 00:10:31.916
Don't do this, don't do that, and so on.

00:10:31.916 --> 00:10:35.516
Nowadays, the models actually are much more smarter.

00:10:35.516 --> 00:10:38.076
So you can actually give positive steering.

00:10:38.076 --> 00:10:44.116
You can specifically say what to do,
the model, what to do, and it actually can follow quite well.

00:10:44.116 --> 00:10:46.996
So you can actually shorten your prompts there.

00:10:46.996 --> 00:10:49.716
The other one is to use progressive disclosure.

00:10:49.956 --> 00:10:53.916
And basically this means don't reveal all your context at once.

00:10:54.116 --> 00:10:56.396
Reveal it gradually, whenever it's needed.

00:10:56.396 --> 00:11:02.916
So this is the same idea that we have,
for example, for skills in cloud code or in Kiro.

00:11:03.116 --> 00:11:07.436
And finally, try to use, for example, some Chrome optimizer.

00:11:07.476 --> 00:11:10.516
There is a built in chrome optimizer in Amazon Bedrock.

00:11:10.556 --> 00:11:16.716
There are also some other open source optimizers,
so pick your selection there.

00:11:18.276 --> 00:11:20.196
The fifth strategy is cost governance.

00:11:20.196 --> 00:11:28.996
And this basically means knowing who is spending what and having
the right controls in order to stop costs from spiraling, right?

00:11:29.596 --> 00:11:32.836
Amazon Bedrock allows you to measure many different metrics.

00:11:32.836 --> 00:11:37.196
And this could be, for example, consumption metrics, latency metrics,

00:11:38.716 --> 00:11:40.516
invocation metrics, and so on.

00:11:41.716 --> 00:11:46.276
It allows you to allocate cost.
And specifically you can tag IAM users.

00:11:46.276 --> 00:11:53.876
You can tag IAM roles, projects,
or even whole applications using application inference profiles.

00:11:54.156 --> 00:11:57.716
You can set up alerts in AWS budgets.

00:11:57.836 --> 00:11:59.796
And also you can have visualizations.

00:11:59.796 --> 00:12:03.156
You can have a bird's eye view of what's happening with your cost.

00:12:03.156 --> 00:12:07.316
There is a built in dashboard in CloudWatch,
or you can create your own.

00:12:07.436 --> 00:12:10.996
So basically Amazon Bedrock gives you full.

00:12:11.316 --> 00:12:15.996
Per app, per team, per per user cost.

00:12:16.036 --> 00:12:16.836
Governance.

00:12:18.596 --> 00:12:23.636
Now let's talk about security.
And this is this makes a difference between a demo

00:12:23.636 --> 00:12:27.236
and a production system. And let's start with the basics.

00:12:27.276 --> 00:12:31.996
Right. Amazon Bedrock is by design secure and compliant.

00:12:32.276 --> 00:12:36.916
All your data is never shared with any model providers.

00:12:37.316 --> 00:12:42.476
All data in transit is always encrypted with

00:12:42.516 --> 00:12:50.316
at least TLS 1.2. All API calls are monitored with AWS CloudTrail,

00:12:50.396 --> 00:12:58.156
there is a very rich portfolio of compliance standards
for example, ISO, SoC, two, HIPAA and so on.

00:12:58.756 --> 00:13:04.596
Customers who are using Bedrock are compliant with GDPR,

00:13:04.876 --> 00:13:11.036
and also Amazon Bedrock is approved for further
up high workloads in the US government.

00:13:11.476 --> 00:13:14.556
So with that out of the way, let's talk about Amazon Bedrock.

00:13:14.556 --> 00:13:20.116
Guardrails. Amazon Bedrock. Guardrails is one
of the features of Amazon Bedrock that allows you

00:13:20.116 --> 00:13:25.436
to implement responsible AI
principles in your Generative AI applications.

00:13:25.796 --> 00:13:33.756
It allows you it gives you six different key safeguards things,
for example, like denied topics,

00:13:35.076 --> 00:13:38.956
blocking, detecting and redacting PII,

00:13:40.436 --> 00:13:44.676
blocked words, prompt attacks, hallucination detection, and so on.

00:13:44.876 --> 00:13:51.036
Now, until now, you were able
to apply these guardrails on AWS account level,

00:13:51.036 --> 00:13:58.716
but now it is possible to apply it in the whole
organization across all AWS accounts of a whole organization.

00:13:58.716 --> 00:14:07.916
You can apply Guardrails per agent,
in knowledge bases or even in models that are not hosted in Bedrock.

00:14:08.076 --> 00:14:11.796
And now it can be also applied for coding use cases.

00:14:14.676 --> 00:14:20.716
Bedrock. Guardrails is very good
for safeguarding your content, but when it comes to agents,

00:14:20.836 --> 00:14:23.916
it's important for us to control their actions, right?

00:14:23.956 --> 00:14:27.516
And this is where Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Policy comes.

00:14:28.076 --> 00:14:33.876
It intercepts any tool call and it
evaluates it against deterministic policies.

00:14:34.076 --> 00:14:40.716
So in real time you're able to control
the actions of your agents, right?

00:14:40.756 --> 00:14:46.676
Basically, for example, what tool they're calling with,
what parameters under what conditions and so on.

00:14:47.076 --> 00:14:51.476
It's very important because again,
this is in real time And it's deterministic.

00:14:51.476 --> 00:14:52.676
It's not probabilistic.

00:14:54.036 --> 00:14:56.836
And finally, Amazon Bedrock AgentCore registry.

00:14:56.836 --> 00:15:00.000
This is our unified catalog for models and tools.

